#usda 1.0
(
    defaultPrim = "MotorClass"
    upAxis = "Y"
    metersPerUnit = 1
)

# STANDARD winding — the fleet default, and the one every measured figure in
# `rocker_bogie.usda` was taken on.
#
# A motor class is a separate file for the same reason a tire is: it is a separate
# DECISION. Re-winding a motor does not change the housing, the mount, the thermal
# model or the electrical connector — only the four numbers below — and two rovers
# carrying the same `motor.usda` may run different windings.
#
# ── WHAT MAKES A WINDING SELF-CONSISTENT ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# All classes in this directory share ONE frame: the same rotor iron, the same
# housing, the same 28 V bus. Within a frame:
#
#   * `stallTorque · noLoadSpeed ≈ const` — a winding trades torque for speed at
#     roughly constant power. τ = k_t·V/R and ω_nl = V/k_e with k_t = k_e, so more
#     turns buys torque and gives away speed in the same proportion.
#     Peak mechanical power is τ_stall·ω_nl/4 ≈ 900 W for every class here.
#   * The frame size is a property of the housing, not the winding. The complete
#     tire-and-drivetrain assembly inertia is authored on the composed wheel and
#     changes only for a different vehicle frame size
#     (τ ∝ s³ but J ∝ s⁵, so a bigger motor's inertia grows much faster than its
#     torque — see `lunokhod.usda`, the one genuinely smaller machine).
#   * `continuousTorque` tracks stall at ~60%, the usual thermal margin, and must
#     never exceed it.
#
# 2400 rad/s is fast for a machine this size — deliberately so. See `motor.usda`
# for why the speed lives in the winding rather than in the gearbox reduction.

def Xform "MotorClass" (
    prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoProgramAPI"]
    kind = "component"
)
{
    float inputs:stall_torque = 1.5
    float inputs:no_load_speed = 2400.0
}
